  "version": "enrolled",
  "operative_text": "(a_in_person_id)\n(section) Section 28 (KRS 117.225(1))\n(quote) Any person desiring to vote on election day shall give his or her name and address to the clerk of the election and shall provide proof of identification as defined in Section 41 of this Act\n(section) Section 30 (KRS 117.227)\n(quote) Except as otherwise provided, election officers shall confirm the identity of each voter by proof of identification as defined in Section 41 of this Act\n(section) Section 41 (KRS 117.375 renumbered KRS 117.001(13), definition)\n(quote) \"Proof of identification\" means a document that was issued by: (a) The United States or the Commonwealth of Kentucky, and the document contains: 1. The name of the individual to whom the document was issued; and 2. A photograph of the individual to whom the document was issued; (b) The United States Department of Defense, a branch of the uniformed services, the Merchant Marine, or the Kentucky National Guard, and the document contains:\n(section) Section 41 (KRS 117.001(13)(c)-(d))\n(quote) (c) A public or private college, university, or postgraduate technical or professional school located within the United States, and the document contains: 1. The name of the individual to whom the document was issued; and 2. A photograph of the individual to whom the document was issued; or (d) Any city government, county government, urban-county government, charter county government, consolidated local government, or unified local government, which is located within this state, and the document contains:\n(section) Section 28 (KRS 117.225(2))\n(quote) A voter who votes in person at a precinct polling place that is located at a state-licensed care facility where the voter resides is not required to provide proof of identification, as defined in Section 41 of this Act[KRS 117.375], before voting in a primary or an election.\n(section) Section 11 (KRS 117.085(2), in-person absentee)\n(quote) The voter who elects to vote in-person absentee shall provide[if the voter provides] proof of identification as defined in Section 41 of this Act[KRS 117.375] or meet[meets] the requirements of KRS 117.228 and 117.229\n(b_no_id_remedy)\n(section) Section 31 (KRS 117.228(1))\n(quote) if a voter is unable to provide proof of identification as required under KRS 117.225, and as defined under Section 41 of this Act\n(section) Section 31 (KRS 117.228(1)(c)8)\n(quote) The voter has one (1) of the following impediments to procure proof of identification as defined in Section 41 of this Act\n(section) Section 31 (KRS 117.228(2))\n(quote) In addition to the requirements of subsection (1) of this section, to cast a ballot, the voter who is unable to provide proof of identification shall provide to an election officer: (a) The voter's Social Security Card; (b) Any identification card issued by a county in this state which has the name of the voter stated and has been approved in writing by the State Board of Elections pursuant to administrative regulations promulgated under KRS Chapter 13A; (c) Any identification card with the voter's photograph and the name of the voter stated; (d) Any food stamp identification card, electronic benefit transfer card, or supplemental nutrition assistance card, that is issued by this state and has the name of the voter stated; or (e) A credit or debit card with the name of the voter stated.\n(section) Section 32 (KRS 117.229)\n(quote) if a voter is unable to provide proof of identification, as required under KRS 117.225 and as defined under Section 41 of this Act\n(c_mail_ballot_id)\n(section) Section 11 (KRS 117.085(1)(a))\n(quote) All requests for a mail-in absentee ballot shall be requested through a secure online portal established by the State Board of Elections, except for: 1. Voters identified in KRS 117.077; 2. Disabled voters; and 3. Covered voters in paragraph (i) of this subsection;\n(section) Section 11 (KRS 117.085(1)(b))\n(quote) Acquiring a mail-in absentee ballot by means of the online portal shall require the voter to input personally identifiable information for verification.\n(section) Section 11 (KRS 117.085(1)(d))\n(quote) The online portal shall have the capacity to ensure the identity of the voter through proof of identification as required under Section 30 of this Act or by means of Section 31 of this Act.\n(section) Section 11 (KRS 117.085, application for voters not using the portal)\n(quote) the voter shall provide a copy of his or her proof of identification, as defined in Section 41 of this Act\n(d_proof_of_citizenship) not stated.\n(e_other_rule)\n(section) Section 31 (KRS 117.228(1)(c)1, voter affirmation)\n(quote) The voter is a citizen of the United States;\n(other) Section 1 (KRS 83A.045): partisan city elections rules.\n(other) Sections 2-5 (KRS 116.045, 116.046, 116.112, 116.113): voter registration methods, high-school registration forms, address-change purge program using Postal Service data, removal on reported death. No ID-number or citizenship matching appears.\n(other) Section 6 (new KRS ch. 117 section): bans knowingly collecting or taking control of another person's ballot (text begins 'No person shall knowingly collect, gain possession of, deliver, or exercise control'; I read only its first lines).\n(other) Sections 7-10 (KRS 117.035, 117.045, 117.055, 117.066): county boards, precinct lists, polling places, vote locations.\n(other) Section 11 (KRS 117.085): absentee voting. Mail-in requests go through a State Board online portal, with the portal opening no more than 45 days before and closing 14 days before an election; in-person absentee window set at the Thursday, Friday and Saturday before the election; a phone/in-person fallback through the county clerk.\n(other) Sections 12-15 (KRS 117.086, 117.0863, 117.087, 117.088): return of absentee ballots including drop boxes, assistance, challenges, blind voters.\n(other) Sections 16-27, 29 (KRS 117.105-117.215, 117.235): voting equipment and systems, ballot preparation, polling-place rules.\n(other) Sections 33-40, 42-46 (KRS 117.245-117.355, 117.377-117.995): oaths, assistance, write-ins, counting, canvass, costs, system approval, penalties.\n(other) Sections 47-71 (KRS 118.015-118.415, 118A, 119, 120, 242.120, 424.290): party, nomination, ballot-order, recount and contest provisions; conforming ballot terms.\n(other) Sections 72-74: new sections (KRS ch. 118, 48 and 65) restricting use of public money to advocate on ballot questions (read only the first lines); Section 75 (KRS 39A.100): emergency powers; Sections 76-86: candidate, residence, property tax notice, contested-election and special-election provisions; Section 87 repeals KRS 117.381 and 117.387 (and others listed).\n(other) version: orig_bill (introduced, BR 1258): carries slots a, b, c, e in the same form as the final text: the proof-of-ID definition (college IDs included), the 117.228 affirmation and secondary-ID list (credit or debit card included), the 117.229 federal provisional ballot, and the online portal that must verify the voter 'through proof of identification as required under Section 30 of this Act or by means of Section 31 of this Act'. I compared the portal paragraph, the ID cross-references and the 117.228 text; I did not diff the whole 7,000-line bill.\n(other) version: bill (furthest = enrolled, header 'HB 574/EN', title adds 'making an appropriation therefor'): same ID slots as the introduced text. In the portal paragraph (1)(f) the final adds that the portal 'shall not be open or permit any mail-in ballot requests to occur more than forty-five (45) days' before an election; other non-ID differences (in-person absentee timing) were seen but not catalogued.\n(other) version: The House and Senate committee substitutes and floor amendments are now saved (see lines below); the conference or concurrence steps, if any, are not separate files.\n(other) version: HCS1 (House committee substitute 1, 21 RS HB 574/HCS 1, saved 2026-11-11): carries slots a, b, c, e with the same ID text as the introduced bill (ID rule and definition in Section 41, 117.228 affirmation and secondary-ID list with credit or debit card, online portal ID check, citizen affirmation). I compared the ID cross-references, the portal paragraph and the 117.228 text, and found no change in them. It differs from the introduced text by dropping 'Wednesday,' from the in-person absentee days and adding an amended KRS 39A.100 (emergency powers) near the end.\n(other) version: HFA2 (House floor amendment 2, saved 2026-11-11): no ID or citizenship text. It adds a new Section 78 amending KRS 116.035 so a homeless voter may choose a fixed-address shelter or other place as his or her residence. Adopted with HCS 1.\n(other) version: HFA3 (House floor amendment 3, saved 2026-11-11, defeated 28-68): no ID or citizenship text. It only changes the in-person absentee voting days on page 29 (adds 'Wednesday,' and 'during the two (2) weeks').\n(other) version: SCS1 (Senate committee substitute 1, 21 RS HB 574/SCS 1, saved 2026-11-11): carries slots a, b, c, e with the same ID text as HCS 1 (checked: ID rule, 117.228 list, portal ID-check paragraph). Against HCS 1 it adds a rule that the portal must not take mail-in requests more than 45 days before an election, and adds an automatic-recount section and recount procedure changes; the ID text does not change.\n(other) version: SFA1 (Senate floor amendment 1, saved 2026-11-11): no ID or citizenship text. It makes a cross-reference to a new subsection (3) of the precinct-consolidation section and adds 'precincts and the consolidation of' (page 24-25 of SCS 1). Adopted with SCS 1.\n(other) note: The bill moves the definitions section: KRS 117.375 is repealed, reenacted, amended and renumbered as KRS 117.001 (Section 41), so every ID cross-reference now reads 'Section 41 of this Act' in place of the bracketed 'KRS 117.375'. The definition of proof of identification itself, the 117.227 / 117.228 / 117.229 text and the credit-or-debit-card item are reprinted unchanged from SB 2 (2020), so slots a, b and e are reprints, not new rules. What IS new for slot c is the online portal for mail-in requests (the old phone, fax, mail, e-mail request wording is bracketed as deleted). A reprinted, deleted (bracketed) copy of the old 117.375 definition (with 'Merchant Marines' wording) also appears further down inside Section 41 and should not be read as current law. Extra details on the in-person absentee ID sentence: it is in 117.085(2).\n(note) Off this topic: the bill sets no ID or citizenship-proof rule.",
